  "narrative": "Compared to last week: Todays sale featured bred heifers, bred cows, and weigh ups. Only a few tested open heifers sold today off bred consignments. Weigh up cows sold on mostly light to moderate demand and mostly moderate offerings. Cow quality this week was mostly average to attractive. Feeding cow demand continues to lighten. Buyers report many feedlots are full. Canadian buyers were mostly out of the market this week which caused cow prices to decline further. Packers continue to put cows away for the upcoming holidays. Many of these offerings are blemished or thin. Feeding cows sold mostly 5.00-10.00 lower on breaking flesh cows, boning and lean fleshed feeding cows sold mostly 2.00-3.00 lower. Slaughter cows sold mostly steady on breaking cows, boning and lean cows sold steady to 3.00 lower. Slaughter bulls sold generally steady in a narrow comparison. Weigh up conditions were mostly average. Young age cows sold 10.00 to 20.00 lower. Demand for cows to rebreed has lightened significantly over the last 2 weeks. Fewer takers were seen for this class of cows. Demand for bred cows was good to very good, especially on high quality cows off dispersions. Older cows continue to be met with moderate to good demand and demand for this age of cows was constant throughout the sale, which stretched late into the evening. A large crowd was on hand for todays Thanksgiving week bred cow special. Market activity was mostly active with rapid bidding noticed throughout the day. Supply included: 1% Feeder Cattle (100% Heifers); 26% Slaughter Cattle (98% Cows, 2% Bulls); 73% Replacement Cattle (11% Stock Cows, 75% Bred Cows, 12% Bred Heifers, 2% Open Heifers). Feeder cattle supply over 600 lbs was 100%.",
